        "title": "Can we really do two things at once? The cost of multitasking",
        "lead": "A phone message, a video in the background and a mathematics problem: feeling very active tells us little about how successfully we have processed information. Psychology distinguishes simultaneously performing simple actions from switching between jobs that require thought. This distinction explains why we sometimes combine activities without difficulty and sometimes lose our train of thought after every notification.",
